} And the compiler immediately stops you: error[E0117]: only traits defined in the current crate can be implemented for types defined outside of the crate This is Rust's well-known Orphan Rule . Once people hit this wall, they usually either complain that the compiler is too rigid or dutifully wrap the type in a NewType. But if you look closely at how the Orphan Rule actually works, it is not a dead end. By design, it leaves a pretty wide door open. What Is the Orphan Rule Actually Protecting Against?

Setting the jargon aside, the logic behind the Orphan Rule is simple: if you want a type to implement a trait, at least one of those two things (the type or the trait) must belong to your own crate. In plain English, you don't get to make rules for someone else's types. Suppose crate A implements ToJson for uuid::Uuid and outputs a hyphenated UUID string, while crate B also implements ToJson for uuid::Uuid and outputs 32 raw hex characters without hyphens. If a project depends on both A and B, the compiler has a problem when it sees uuid.to_json() : which implementation should it pick? To prevent this kind of global conflict, Rust enforces coherence , and the Orphan Rule is how it gets enforced. There is nothing wrong with the rule itself. It keeps the Rust ecosystem from turning into a mess as dependency trees grow. The catch is that real-world projects constantly run into a very common need: making a third-party type implement a trait from another third-party crate. Neither the type nor the trait is yours, yet gluing libraries together like this happens all the time. So what can you do? Why the Two Usual Workarounds Fall Short

Right now, the Rust ecosystem mostly relies on two workarounds. Both appear to solve the problem on the surface, but because neither addresses the root cause, they get awkward fast. 1. Building it into the library

Many foundational crates take this route: since users can't write the impl themselves, the crate provides it internally. Because ToJson is defined inside the crate, implementing it for any external type satisfies the Orphan Rule. Open up the Cargo.toml of one of these crates, and you will see a long list of feature flags: with-uuid , with-chrono , with-time , with-jiff , with-url , with-rust-decimal , and so on. It looks helpful at first, but it tries to enumerate an open, constantly changing ecosystem inside a single crate . That approach has clear limits, and the downsides show up quickly: You can never support everything. Today the community favors chrono , tomorrow people switch to jiff , and next week your team builds its own internal Money type. If the library doesn't have built-in support, you are stuck waiting for a release or opening a PR yourself. Version lock-in. When a third-party type crate bumps its major version, your whole dependency tree gets stuck until the trait crate publishes a matching update. Hardcoded behavior. Maybe the library serializes DateTime<Utc> as an ISO-8601 string by default, while your API spec requires an integer timestamp in milliseconds. Because the implementation lives inside the library, you have no way to change it. 2. Wrap it in a NewType yourself

